<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#olu-of-warri">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olu of Warri</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Olu of Warri is the title of the king of the Itsekiri people of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. The line traces its origin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ginuwa">Ginuwa</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> prince of the late 15th century, and its traditional seat is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ode-itsekiri">Ode-Itsekiri</a>. After the death of Olu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akengbuwa">Akengbuwa</a> in 1848 the throne stood vacant until the kingship was restored in 1936, and the Olu remains a traditional ruler today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olu-of-warri.jpg" alt="Olu of Warri" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Nicolas Bellin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1765_Bonne_Map_of_West_Africa,_the_Gulf_of_Guinea,_and_Benin_-_Geographicus_-_GulfofGuinee-bellin-1765.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
